# No external networking without permission

Get explicit permission in the current user message before any external network access. Permission from an earlier turn does not carry forward. A standing "you may ssh" from a previous message is not permission for this one.

External networking includes SSH, SCP, rsync to a remote host, curl or wget to a host the user did not already ask you to reach, AWS CLI calls, git push, git fetch, and opening a socket to a machine that is not this one. Reading local connection files is allowed. Using them is not.

When the user does give permission, do only the action they named. Read permission is not permission to write, install, delete, restart, or deploy. If a command would change the remote machine, stop and ask.

The Chorus EC2 key and SSH config live in `/data/mjma/chorus/aws-connection`. Having those files is not permission to connect.

```bash
# ❌ BAD — connects because the key is on disk
ssh -i /data/mjma/chorus/aws-connection/voices_smiling_key.pem ec2-user@<host> 'df -h'

# ✅ GOOD — the current message says "you may ssh and only run df"
ssh -i /data/mjma/chorus/aws-connection/voices_smiling_key.pem ec2-user@<host> 'df -h'
```
